---
// Publish a module (design: RegistryPublish). Mostly prose: how a module gets
// into the registry. There is one path: a pull request to doze-modules, tested
// by CI, then built on merge and signed by doze-registry's CI. Only the doze
// namespace exists, and doze loads modules from nowhere else.
import RegistryBar from "../../components/RegistryBar.astro";
import Page from "../../layouts/Page.astro";
import { hrefFor } from "../../routes";

const contributing = "https://github.com/doze-dev/doze-modules/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md";
const unit = "go vet ./...\ngo test -race ./...";
const accept =
  "scripts/backend-env.sh valkey aarch64-apple-darwin > /tmp/valkey.env\nset -a; . /tmp/valkey.env; set +a\ngo test -tags acceptance -v -timeout 20m ./modules/valkey/...";
---
<Page title="Publish a module · Registry" theme="light" current="registry">
<div class="pb">
<RegistryBar section="publish" />

<section class="pb-wrap" style="padding-top: 40px">
  <nav aria-label="Breadcrumb" style="font-size: 13px; color: #6E5F79; margin-bottom: 18px"><a href={hrefFor("Registry.dc.html")}>Registry</a> / Publish a module</nav>
  <h1 class="pb-h1" style="margin: 0; font-family: var(--font-display); font-weight: 800; font-size: 46px; letter-spacing: -0.03em; line-height: 1.02">Publish a module</h1>
  <p class="pb-body" style="font-size: 18px; margin-top: 14px">A module teaches doze to run one more engine. Every module in the registry is written in <a href="https://github.com/doze-dev/doze-modules">doze-modules</a>, arrives as a pull request, and is built and signed by CI after the merge. Here is that path, with the rules the registry enforces.</p>

  <div style="margin-top: 26px; display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(300px, 100%), 1fr)); gap: 0; border: 1px solid #E0A458; border-radius: 16px; overflow: hidden; background: #FFFFFF">
    <div style="padding: 22px 24px; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 10px; background: #FFF4E8">
      <span class="pb-kick" style="color: #6B3A06">Status, honestly</span>
      <p style="font-size: 17px; color: #140C1B; font-weight: 600; line-height: 1.45">doze-dev publishes every module in the registry. Contributions arrive as pull requests to doze-modules.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 14.5px; color: #4A3D55">doze has one registry and one namespace, <span class="pb-mono">doze</span>, and loads modules from nowhere else. Any other namespace is refused: <span class="pb-mono" style="font-size: 13px; color: #B4233C">module namespace "acme": doze has one registry, and its modules are all under "doze"</span></p>
    </div>
    <div style="padding: 22px 24px; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 12px">
      <span class="pb-kick">Two ways to run an engine</span>
      <div style="display: flex; gap: 12px"><span class="pb-chip pb-c-good" style="flex: none">module</span><p style="font-size: 14.5px; color: #4A3D55"><b style="color: #140C1B">Contribute it to doze-modules.</b> A pull request to <a href="https://github.com/doze-dev/doze-modules">doze-modules</a>; once merged, CI builds it and the registry signs it as an official module.</p></div>
      <div style="display: flex; gap: 12px"><span class="pb-chip pb-c-dim" style="flex: none">service</span><p style="font-size: 14.5px; color: #4A3D55"><b style="color: #140C1B">Anything else runs as your own service.</b> <span class="pb-ic">service "mailpit" {"{"} run = "mailpit" {"}"}</span> in doze.hcl runs any command you can start from a shell; give it a <span class="pb-mono">port</span> and it sleeps until something connects.</p></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="pb-wrap pb-layout" style="padding-bottom: 80px">
  <nav class="pb-toc" data-pb-toc aria-label="On this page" style="padding-top: 56px">
    <span class="pb-kick" style="padding: 0 0 8px 0">On this page</span>
    <a class="pb-cur" href="#what">What a module is</a>
    <a href="#write">Write it in doze-modules</a>
    <a href="#describe">Describe: your page</a>
    <a href="#testing">Testing</a>
    <a href="#release">Releasing</a>
    <a href="#oldest">The oldest doze it needs</a>
    <a href="#review">The review checklist</a>
    <a href="#path">From pull request to registry</a>
    <a href="#checklist">Pull request checklist</a>
  </nav>

  <div style="min-width: 0">
    <!-- 1 -->
    <section class="pb-sec" id="what" aria-labelledby="h-what">
      <span class="pb-num">01</span>
      <h2 id="h-what">What a module is</h2>
      <p class="pb-body">A plugin binary, written in Go with <a href="https://github.com/doze-dev/doze-sdk">doze-sdk</a>, that speaks the doze plugin protocol over gRPC. doze is an engine-blind host: proxy, supervisor, config, lockfile. Everything engine-specific lives in the module: how to find the engine binary, set up its data directory, start it, tell when it is ready, and what its config block accepts. This is the Terraform provider model, with one difference: every module comes from the one signed registry.</p>
      <div class="pb-grid2">
        <div class="pb-card"><h3>Self-contained</h3><p>The plugin is the server. doze AWS and doze Kafka work this way. No <span class="pb-mono">version =</span>.</p></div>
        <div class="pb-card"><h3>Real upstream engine</h3><p>The module fetches a real binary (Postgres, Valkey) from doze-binaries and declares the engine majors it supports.</p></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 2 -->
    <section class="pb-sec" id="write" aria-labelledby="h-write">
      <span class="pb-num">02</span>
      <h2 id="h-write">Write it in doze-modules</h2>
      <p class="pb-body">A module is not its own repo: it is a package under <span class="pb-mono">modules/&lt;name&gt;/</span> in <a href="https://github.com/doze-dev/doze-modules">doze-modules</a> and a line in <span class="pb-mono">modules.yaml</span>. Copy the smallest one, <span class="pb-mono">modules/valkey/</span>. Before writing code, open a "Propose a module" issue: the engine, why, its licence, where its binaries come from, and who will maintain it.</p>
      <div class="pb-code"><div class="pb-code-bar"><span>What a module is made of</span></div><pre>modules/&lt;name&gt;/&lt;name&gt;.go         <span class="pb-c"># the driver: Type, Resolve, Provision, Plan, …</span>
modules/&lt;name&gt;/config.go         <span class="pb-c"># the HCL block it decodes</span>
modules/&lt;name&gt;/describe.go       <span class="pb-c"># Describe(): docs and engine support, from code</span>
modules/&lt;name&gt;/config_test.go    <span class="pb-c"># decode tests</span>
modules/&lt;name&gt;/acceptance_test.go <span class="pb-c"># boots the real backend (-tags acceptance)</span>
modules/&lt;name&gt;/plugin/main.go    <span class="pb-c"># the plugin binary: dozeplugin.Main(Driver{}, &amp;Config{})</span></pre></div>
      <div class="pb-grid2">
        <div class="pb-card"><span class="pb-chip pb-c-warn" style="align-self: flex-start">first</span><h3>The backend in doze-binaries</h3><p>Users download engine binaries from <a href="https://github.com/doze-dev/doze-binaries">doze-binaries</a>, and so does the release gate. A new engine needs a build recipe there, for all three platforms, before its module can release.</p></div>
        <div class="pb-card"><span class="pb-chip pb-c-dim" style="align-self: flex-start">register</span><h3>Four lines elsewhere</h3><p>The version in <span class="pb-mono">modules.yaml</span>, the driver in <span class="pb-mono">cmd/dzm</span>'s <span class="pb-mono">describers</span>, a backend in <span class="pb-mono">scripts/backend-env.sh</span>, and the module in the acceptance workflow's matrix.</p></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 3 -->
    <section class="pb-sec" id="describe" aria-labelledby="h-describe">
      <span class="pb-num">03</span>
      <h2 id="h-describe">Describe: what your page shows comes from code</h2>
      <p class="pb-body">Hand-written module docs rot, so everything a user sees is generated from your driver's <span class="pb-ic">Describe()</span>. doze-modules' build tool refuses to package a module without it, and its tests fail when one is incomplete: no title, no documented config, no example block, no engine versions.</p>
      <div class="pb-panel" style="overflow: hidden">
        <table class="pb-tbl pb-tbl-stack">
          <thead><tr><th style="width: 30%">On the module page</th><th style="width: 30%">From</th><th>Note</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Name, one line, paragraph</td><td data-l="From" class="pb-mono" style="font-size: 13px">Title · Tagline · Description</td><td data-l="Note">Shown on the card in search and in the header.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Category and port</td><td data-l="From" class="pb-mono" style="font-size: 13px">Category · Port</td><td data-l="Note">database, cache, queue, storage, workflow, other.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Engines, and the gate</td><td data-l="From" class="pb-mono" style="font-size: 13px">Versions</td><td data-l="Note"><b style="color: #140C1B">Signed.</b> Becomes the index's <span class="pb-mono">engines</span>; a user declaring an unlisted major is stopped before your code runs.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>The example block</td><td data-l="From" class="pb-mono" style="font-size: 13px">Example · ExampleLabel</td><td data-l="Note">Must run as written: it is the first thing people paste.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Reference tab</td><td data-l="From" class="pb-mono" style="font-size: 13px">Config · Blocks</td><td data-l="Note"><span class="pb-mono">Since</span>/<span class="pb-mono">Until</span> render "engine ≥ 18" badges and need a matching check in your decoder.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Start, recipes, env, connect</td><td data-l="From" class="pb-mono" style="font-size: 13px">[Start · Recipes · Env · Connect]</td><td data-l="Note"><span class="pb-chip pb-c-info">new</span> Snippets for the Overview tab: first start, common setups, the variables it exports, how to connect. Not fields in <span class="pb-mono">engine.Description</span> yet.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 4 -->
    <section class="pb-sec" id="testing" aria-labelledby="h-testing">
      <span class="pb-num">04</span>
      <h2 id="h-testing">Testing, against the real engine</h2>
      <p class="pb-body">Three layers, each catching what the one before cannot. Run the first two on your machine before you open the pull request; CI runs all three.</p>
      <div class="pb-grid2">
        <div class="pb-card"><span class="pb-chip pb-c-dim" style="align-self: flex-start">every pull request</span><h3>Decode tests</h3><p>Defaults, error messages, version gates, that an unknown attribute is an error, and that every <span class="pb-mono">Describe()</span> is complete. On Linux and macOS.</p></div>
        <div class="pb-card"><span class="pb-chip pb-c-dim" style="align-self: flex-start">-tags acceptance</span><h3>enginetest</h3><p>Boots a real engine from your driver and checks that each declared option is applied in the running server. Skips without <span class="pb-mono">DOZE_&lt;ENGINE&gt;_BINDIR</span>.</p></div>
        <div class="pb-card"><span class="pb-chip pb-c-dim" style="align-self: flex-start">before release</span><h3>The release gate</h3><p>After the merge, CI runs acceptance again against the backend users download from doze-binaries. No version publishes without it.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="pb-code"><div class="pb-code-bar"><span>Unit tests, as the pull request runs them</span><button class="pb-copy" type="button" data-copy={unit}>Copy</button></div><pre><span class="pb-p">$</span> go vet ./...
<span class="pb-p">$</span> go test -race ./...</pre></div>
      <div class="pb-code"><div class="pb-code-bar"><span>Acceptance, with the published backend</span><button class="pb-copy" type="button" data-copy={accept}>Copy</button></div><pre><span class="pb-p">$</span> scripts/backend-env.sh valkey aarch64-apple-darwin &gt; /tmp/valkey.env   <span class="pb-c"># needs curl, tar, yq</span>
<span class="pb-p">$</span> set -a; . /tmp/valkey.env; set +a                                      <span class="pb-c"># DOZE_VALKEY_BINDIR=…</span>
<span class="pb-p">$</span> go test -tags acceptance -v -timeout 20m ./modules/valkey/...</pre></div>
    </section>

    <!-- 5 -->
    <section class="pb-sec" id="release" aria-labelledby="h-release">
      <span class="pb-num">05</span>
      <h2 id="h-release">Releasing: archives, hashes, signatures</h2>
      <p class="pb-body">You don't release anything. On the merge, doze-modules' CI builds the archives with <span class="pb-mono">dzm</span> (a thin loop over the SDK's <span class="pb-mono">modtool</span>) and uploads them to the module's GitHub release. The registry signs them, because the key lives there.</p>
      <div class="pb-code"><div class="pb-code-bar"><span>What CI builds for valkey 0.2.2</span></div><pre><span class="pb-c">dist/valkey/</span>
  valkey-plugin-0.2.2-aarch64-apple-darwin.tar.gz
  valkey-plugin-0.2.2-aarch64-unknown-linux-gnu.tar.gz
  valkey-plugin-0.2.2-x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu.tar.gz
  index.yaml   <span class="pb-c"># protocol, engines, doze, sha256 per archive; unsigned</span>
  meta.yaml    <span class="pb-c"># the docs, from Describe()</span></pre></div>
      <div class="pb-grid2">
        <div class="pb-card"><h3>Bump for every change</h3><p>Users' locks pin releases. A fix, a new argument, a new engine major: each is a new version in <span class="pb-mono">modules.yaml</span>. Only a changed version releases.</p></div>
        <div class="pb-card"><h3>Never rebuild a release</h3><p>Rebuilding a published version with different bytes is refused:</p><code class="pb-mono" style="font-size: 13px; color: #B4233C; overflow-wrap: anywhere">valkey 0.2.2 (aarch64-apple-darwin) is already published with a different sha256 — bump the module version instead of rebuilding it</code></div>
        <div class="pb-card"><h3>Every old release stays</h3><p>CI merges the published <span class="pb-mono">index.yaml</span> before building, so last year's locks and older doze versions keep resolving. The registry's CI refuses to drop one.</p></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 6 -->
    <section class="pb-sec" id="oldest" aria-labelledby="h-oldest">
      <span class="pb-num">06</span>
      <h2 id="h-oldest">Declaring the oldest doze your release needs</h2>
      <p class="pb-body">The plugin protocol number says the wire is the same. It does not say which doze has everything your module leans on. Each release can carry a <span class="pb-ic">doze</span> field: the oldest doze that runs it.</p>
      <div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(300px, 100%), 1fr)); gap: 14px; align-items: start">
        <div class="pb-code"><div class="pb-code-bar"><span>index.yaml · one release</span></div><pre>releases:
  "0.2.5":
    protocol: 1
    engines: ["14", "15", "16", "17", "18"]
    <span class="pb-hl">doze: "0.2.0"</span>        <span class="pb-c"># oldest doze that runs it</span>
    artifacts: …</pre></div>
        <div class="pb-card" style="gap: 10px">
          <h3>Where the value comes from</h3>
          <p>You don't type it. <span class="pb-mono">modtool</span> stamps it from the SDK you built with (<span class="pb-mono">proto.MinHost</span>). doze-sdk v0.11.0 and later stamp <span class="pb-mono">"0.2.0"</span>: modules built with it leave fetching engine binaries to doze, which only doze 0.2.0 and later do.</p>
          <p style="font-size: 13px; color: #6E5F79">Format is three numbers. <span class="pb-mono">npm run validate</span> rejects anything else: <span class="pb-mono">doze must be the oldest doze version that runs it, as "0.2.0"</span></p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <h3 style="margin-top: 6px">How doze uses it</h3>
      <div class="pb-grid2">
        <div class="pb-card"><span class="pb-chip pb-c-dim" style="align-self: flex-start">choosing a release</span><p>Releases that need a newer doze are skipped; an older release that fits is chosen instead. If none fits, doze says so:</p><code class="pb-mono" style="font-size: 13px; color: #140C1B; background: #FFF4E8; border-radius: 8px; padding: 8px 10px; overflow-wrap: anywhere">postgres 17 needs doze 0.3.0 or newer; this doze is 0.2.0. Update doze (in the app: Settings › About), then run the command again</code><span style="font-size: 13px; color: #6E5F79">doze 0.2 and later. The 0.3.0 release is an example, to show the shape.</span></div>
        <div class="pb-card"><span class="pb-chip pb-c-dim" style="align-self: flex-start">reading doze.lock</span><p>The pin keeps the field, so a teammate on an older doze is told why rather than failing at launch:</p><code class="pb-mono" style="font-size: 13px; color: #140C1B; background: #FFF4E8; border-radius: 8px; padding: 8px 10px; overflow-wrap: anywhere">module doze/postgres 0.3.1 (pinned in doze.lock) needs doze 0.3.0 or newer; this doze is 0.2.0. Update doze (in the app: Settings › About); the lock was written by a newer doze</code><span style="font-size: 13px; color: #6E5F79">doze 0.2 and later. doze 0.1.1 cannot read the field: update doze before <span class="pb-mono">doze modules upgrade</span>.</span></div>
      </div>
      <h3 style="margin-top: 6px">When to raise it</h3>
      <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px; font-size: 15px; color: #4A3D55; line-height: 1.7; max-width: 70ch">
        <li>doze-modules moves to a doze-sdk whose <span class="pb-mono">MinHost</span> is higher: it rises on its own. Say so in the pull request.</li>
        <li>The module starts using a newer protocol feature or SDK field that an older doze ignores or lacks. [An override in <span class="pb-mono">modtool.Module</span> for this case does not exist yet] <span class="pb-chip pb-c-info">new</span></li>
        <li>Never lower it on a published release; it is part of the release, like its bytes.</li>
      </ul>
      <p style="font-size: 13.5px; color: #6E5F79">postgres 0.2.5 and the other 3 Nov 2026 releases are the first to carry it ("0.2.0"); earlier releases have no field and run on any doze. Today it sits outside the signed part of the index; the design moves it inside (<a href={hrefFor("RegistryTrust.dc.html#gaps")}>known gaps</a>).</p>
    </section>

    <!-- 7 -->
    <section class="pb-sec" id="review" aria-labelledby="h-review">
      <span class="pb-num">07</span>
      <h2 id="h-review">The review checklist</h2>
      <p class="pb-body">Each module page shows these as ticks. Some are enforced by tooling today; the rest are checked by a maintainer in review until CI can do it.</p>
      <div class="pb-panel" style="overflow: hidden">
        <table class="pb-tbl pb-tbl-stack">
          <thead><tr><th style="width: 28%">Check</th><th>How</th><th style="width: 20%">Today</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Signed</td><td data-l="How">Every archive and the index verify against the registry key. <span class="pb-mono">npm run validate</span> on every PR and push.</td><td data-l="Today"><span class="pb-chip pb-c-good">enforced</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td>Never rebuilt, never dropped</td><td data-l="How">modtool refuses changed bytes; <span class="pb-mono">validate --against</span> refuses a removed or edited release.</td><td data-l="Today"><span class="pb-chip pb-c-good">enforced</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td>Three platforms</td><td data-l="How">macOS arm64, Linux arm64, Linux x86_64. Built by default; not yet a gate in validate.</td><td data-l="Today"><span class="pb-chip pb-c-warn">built, not gated</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td>Conformance on each engine major</td><td data-l="How">Acceptance tests green before every release, and weekly. Today on one engine major per module, not yet every major in <span class="pb-mono">Versions</span>.</td><td data-l="Today"><span class="pb-chip pb-c-warn">gated, one major</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td>Examples parse</td><td data-l="How">Every example on the page parsed by the current doze in CI.</td><td data-l="Today"><span class="pb-chip pb-c-info">new</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td>Describe is complete</td><td data-l="How">doze-modules' tests: every module has a well-formed <span class="pb-mono">Describe()</span>.</td><td data-l="Today"><span class="pb-chip pb-c-good">enforced</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td>Licence</td><td data-l="How">doze-modules is Apache-2.0; the engine's own licence must let doze download and run it.</td><td data-l="Today"><span class="pb-chip pb-c-dim">review</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td>Source</td><td data-l="How">Public source in doze-modules, linked from the page [to the exact commit of each release: new].</td><td data-l="Today"><span class="pb-chip pb-c-dim">review</span></td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 8 -->
    <section class="pb-sec" id="path" aria-labelledby="h-path">
      <span class="pb-num">08</span>
      <h2 id="h-path">From pull request to registry</h2>
      <p class="pb-body">Nothing after the merge is manual. Nobody outside CI holds the signing key, and doze-dev never signs a module by hand.</p>
      <ol class="pb-flow" style="margin: 0; padding: 0">
        <li><b>Propose</b><span>A "Propose a module" issue on doze-modules: engine, why, licence, backend, maintainer.</span></li>
        <li><b>Backend</b><span>The engine's binaries published in doze-binaries for all three platforms.</span></li>
        <li><b>Pull request</b><span>The module under <span class="pb-mono">modules/&lt;name&gt;/</span>, its version in <span class="pb-mono">modules.yaml</span>, the pull request checklist ticked.</span></li>
        <li><b>CI tests</b><span><span class="pb-mono">go vet</span> and <span class="pb-mono">go test -race</span> on Linux and macOS; a maintainer reviews.</span></li>
        <li><b>Merge, then the gate</b><span>Acceptance against the published backend; then <span class="pb-mono">dzm</span> builds and uploads the release.</span></li>
        <li style="background: #FFFFFF; border-style: dashed"><b>Signed and live</b><span>doze-registry's CI signs it with the doze key, validates it and deploys. <span class="pb-mono">doze modules upgrade</span> picks it up.</span></li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <!-- 9 -->
    <section class="pb-sec" id="checklist" aria-labelledby="h-checklist">
      <span class="pb-num">09</span>
      <div class="pb-print">
        <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center; gap: 12px; flex-wrap: wrap">
          <div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 4px"><span class="pb-kick">Print me</span><h2 id="h-checklist">Pull request checklist</h2></div>
          <button class="pb-btn pb-btn-s" type="button" data-pb-print><svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 9V3h12v6M6 18H4v-7h16v7h-2M8 14h8v7H8z"/></svg>Print</button>
        </div>
        <ul>
          <li><label><input type="checkbox" /><span>Module version bumped in modules.yaml<small>Every change is a new release; an unchanged version does not release.</small></span></label></li>
          <li><label><input type="checkbox" /><span>Describe() updated with the change<small>Source, title, tagline, category, config, Versions, Example.</small></span></label></li>
          <li><label><input type="checkbox" /><span>Example converges as written<small>Acceptance boots it; it is the first thing people paste.</small></span></label></li>
          <li><label><input type="checkbox" /><span>New module registered<small>cmd/dzm describers; README table via <span class="pb-mono">go run ./cmd/dzm readme</span>.</small></span></label></li>
          <li><label><input type="checkbox" /><span>In the acceptance matrix<small>acceptance.yml, and a case in <span class="pb-mono">scripts/backend-env.sh</span>.</small></span></label></li>
          <li><label><input type="checkbox" /><span>Backend in doze-binaries<small>All three platforms; the release gate fetches it.</small></span></label></li>
          <li><label><input type="checkbox" /><span>Unit tests green<small><span class="pb-mono">go vet ./...</span> and <span class="pb-mono">go test -race ./...</span></small></span></label></li>
